From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 13:07:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 21/29] fix(deacon): make the queue-starvation-check able to
 see a queue (gcp-wzhy)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The queue-starvation-check step exists to catch a session whose poll filter
resolves to an identity that doesn't match its own — the refinery self-polled
for 13h42m with seven beads queued and no visible work signal. The step's own
query had the same class of bug, so it reported a flat zero queue for every
session: the one check whose entire job is catching a wrong queue count was
itself producing a wrong queue count.

Three defects, each independently sufficient to zero the result. It enumerated
sessions with `gc agents list`, which yields only the agent alias, then filtered
beads by that single string — but a session is addressed by at least three
identities depending on what assigned the work (alias on molecule wisps, session
bead id on mail, session name on polecat claims). It omitted `--include-infra`,
without which wisps, molecules, sessions and messages are invisible — nearly
everything a coordination session is ever assigned. And it read the hq/city
store as `gc bd --rig=<name>`, a form that fails with `rig not found` while
exiting 0, silently contributing an empty queue; the city must be read with a
bare `gc bd list`, iterating only non-hq rigs.

The corrected query reads the actual assignee strings out of the bead data and
joins them against every identity form `gc session list` reports, matching
exactly via `$ids | index($a)`. The obvious-looking `[.assignee] | inside($ids)`
is wrong: jq's `inside` reduces to `contains`, which on strings is substring
matching, so assignee `gc-t` would match session id `gc-ttk`.

This text is not new — it has been running in the city-local shadow at
~/gc/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml since city commit 3702456. That shadow is a
whole-file override, and the city-local formulas dir is the highest-precedence
layer, so it froze the file against every unrelated upstream change: the pin
move a4be2064 -> fe7e0d3 silently reverted gascity-packs#241's wisp-reconcile
fix, caught only by the pack-drift-local tripwire and folded back by hand.
Upstreaming the block makes the pack a strict superset of the shadow so the
shadow can be deleted rather than hand-folded again.

The orchestration contract in scripts/gascity_pack_inference_gate.py pinned the
naive query verbatim as a required fragment, so it had to move with the formula.
Rather than drop the anchor, it now pins the three properties that were broken —
`gc session list` enumeration, the bare `gc bd list --include-infra` read, and
the exact-match `$ids | index($a)` — so a regression to any single defect fails
the gate instead of silently restoring the zero-queue behaviour.

Validation: gofumpt/go vet/go build/go test -race and golangci-lint clean;
python3 validate_registry.py ok; `make test-patch-guard` 7/7; pytest 106 passed.
The formula parses as TOML with all 8 steps intact.

Deleting the city-local shadow and dropping its check_fork watch from
pack-drift-local.sh is the mayor's half, after this merges and the pin moves.
Pre-existing and NOT touched here: tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py fails at
fork point fe7e0d3 on gastown/tests/test_merge_approval_gate.sh; filed as
gcp-gfjg (discovered-from gcp-wzhy).
---
 gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml | 55 ++++++++++++++++++++++---
 scripts/gascity_pack_inference_gate.py  | 14 ++++++-
 2 files changed, 62 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml b/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml
index cae6ef2..033cfb6 100644
--- a/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml
+++ b/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml
@@ -205,15 +205,58 @@ closes that gap.
 - `A` = count of open beads assigned to the session's agent identity
 - `B` = the most recent bead.updated_at across those beads (work signal)
 
+Read the ACTUAL assignee strings out of the bead data and join them against
+every identity form a session carries. Never pre-filter the naive way —
+
+    gc bd list --status=open --assignee=<one guessed identity>
+
+— because a session is addressed by at least three
+different strings depending on what assigned the work: the agent alias
+(`gastown.deacon`, on molecule wisps), the session bead id (`gc-ttk`, on mail
+messages), and the session name (`gastown__polecat-gc-qkw`, on polecat
+claims). Filtering by any single one silently reports zero — which is the
+very failure mode this step exists to catch.
+
+Match identities EXACTLY. `[.assignee] | inside($ids)` looks right and is not:
+jq's `inside` reduces to `contains`, which on strings is substring matching, so
+assignee `gc-t` matches session id `gc-ttk` and `deacon` matches
+`gastown.deacon`. A step whose whole job is catching a wrong queue count must
+not report a wrong queue count. `$ids | index($a)` is the exact-match form.
+
+Two more things this query gets right, both of which returned a flat zero for
+every session before:
+- `--include-infra` is REQUIRED. Wisps, molecules, sessions and messages live
+  in the wisps table and are invisible without it — that is nearly everything
+  a coordination session is ever assigned.
+- The hq/city store is NOT addressable as `gc bd --rig=<name>`, even though
+  `gc rig list` reports it. That form fails with `rig "<name>" not found` AND
+  exits 0, so it silently contributes an empty queue. Read the city with a
+  bare `gc bd list` and iterate only non-hq rigs.
+
 ```bash
-gc agents list --json --active | jq -r '.[] | .agent_id' | while read -r AGENT; do
-    ASSIGNED=$(gc bd list --status=open --assignee="$AGENT" --json --limit=0 | jq 'length')
-    LATEST=$(gc bd list --status=open --assignee="$AGENT" --json --limit=0 \\
-        | jq -r '[.[].updated_at] | max // ""')
-    printf '%s\\t%s\\t%s\\n' "$AGENT" "$ASSIGNED" "$LATEST"
-done
+QUEUES=$(
+  { gc bd list --status=open --include-infra --json --limit=0
+    for RIG in $(gc rig list --json | jq -r '.rigs[] | select(.hq != true) | .name'); do
+        gc bd --rig="$RIG" list --status=open --include-infra --json --limit=0
+    done
+  } | jq -s 'add | map(select((.assignee // "") != ""))'
+)
+
+# Fail closed: an empty $QUEUES makes --argjson a parse error, not an empty
+# queue report.
+printf '%s' "$QUEUES" | jq -e 'type == "array"' >/dev/null 2>&1 || QUEUES='[]'
+
+gc session list --state active --json | jq -r --argjson q "$QUEUES" '
+  .sessions[] as $s
+  | [ $s.agent_name, ($s.id // ""), ($s.alias // ""), ($s.session_name // "") ] as $ids
+  | ($q | map(select(.assignee as $a | $ids | index($a)))) as $mine
+  | [ $s.agent_name, ($mine | length), ($mine | map(.updated_at) | max // "") ]
+  | @tsv'
 ```
 
+Any assignee in `$QUEUES` that matches no active session is orphaned work —
+a queue whose owner is gone. Worth a look in the same pass.
+
 The latest bead.updated_at is the cheapest proxy for "did this session
 move work recently?" — every claim, status transition, metadata update,
 or note bumps it.
diff --git a/scripts/gascity_pack_inference_gate.py b/scripts/gascity_pack_inference_gate.py
index c072189..de23a53 100644
--- a/scripts/gascity_pack_inference_gate.py
+++ b/scripts/gascity_pack_inference_gate.py
@@ -151,7 +151,19 @@ GASTOWN_BUILD_WORKFLOW_CONTRACTS = {
     "mol-deacon-patrol": (
         "Work-layer health",
         "queue-starvation-check",
-        "gc agents list --json --active",
+        # The starvation check's own query is the thing most able to fail
+        # silently: each of these three anchors replaces a defect that made the
+        # step report a flat zero queue for every session (gcp-wzhy). Session
+        # enumeration must come from `gc session list`, which carries every
+        # identity form an assignee can take — `gc agents list` yields only the
+        # alias. The city/hq store answers to a bare `gc bd list` and NOT to
+        # `--rig=<name>`, which fails while exiting 0. And `--include-infra` is
+        # what makes wisps, molecules, sessions and messages visible at all.
+        "gc session list --state active --json",
+        "gc bd list --status=open --include-infra --json --limit=0",
+        # Exact match, not jq's `inside`/`contains` substring semantics, which
+        # matched `gc-t` against session id `gc-ttk`.
+        "$ids | index($a)",
         "gc bd create --type=task --label=warrant",
         "\"gc.routed_to\":\"{{binding_prefix}}dog\"",
     ),
